# Paper: Java Language Specification §5.6 — Numeric Promotions

**Authors:** James Gosling, Bill Joy, Guy Steele, Gilad Bracha, Alex Buckley  
**Year:** 1996–2025 (continuously updated)  
**Link:** https://docs.oracle.com/javase/specs/jls/se25/html/jls-5.html  
**Domain:** type-systems | java-spec

---

## 1. Why I pulled this

Instructor (Devin Madeley) explained type promotion in discussion reply. I wanted the primary source to verify and go deeper. Questions:
- How does Java handle type promotion when mixing int and double?
- What gets truncated vs promoted automatically?

---

## 2. Core claims (plain English)

- When binary operators mix numeric types, Java promotes to the "widest" type
- Promotion hierarchy: `byte → short → int → long → float → double`
- Promotion is automatic (implicit) and preserves magnitude
- Narrowing requires explicit cast and truncates (no rounding)
- Even `byte + byte = int` (everything promotes to at least int in expressions)

---

## 3. Mechanisms / models

**Promotion Rules (in order):**
```
if (either operand is double) → both become double
else if (either operand is float) → both become float  
else if (either operand is long) → both become long
else → both become int
```

**Widening Primitive Conversions (19 total):**
```
byte → short → int → long → float → double
char → int → long → float → double
```

**Narrowing (explicit cast required):**
```
double → float → long → int → short → byte
```

---

## 4. What this connects to

| Model | Connection |
|-------|------------|
| Cube model | Face hierarchy D→B→L→R→F→U maps to byte→short→int→long→float→double |
| Type lattice | Directed acyclic graph, widening = following edges, narrowing = reversing |
| CLI journey | Each skill level = wider state space you can reason about |
| Bloom operators | Understanding promotion rules = Comprehension; building cube model = Synthesis |

---

## 5. Frictions / doubts

- JLS says widening "preserves magnitude" but SEI CERT NUM52-J says int→float can lose precision
- Resolution: magnitude preserved, but mantissa bits can be lost (23-bit float mantissa < 32-bit int)
- This is the "parity error" — move looks safe but corrupts hidden state

---

## 6. Artifacts spawned

| Type | Path | Description |
|------|------|-------------|
| code | `/artifacts/experiments/TypePromotionCube.java` | Executable demo of all promotion rules |
| diagram | `/maps/type-lattice.md` | Visual hierarchy of Java numeric types |
| annotation | `/annotations/2026-01-type-promotion-cube.md` | Full writeup connecting JLS to cube model |

---

## 7. One-sentence compression

> Java automatically promotes smaller numeric types to larger ones in expressions (widening), but narrowing requires explicit casts and truncates without rounding.

---

